---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ListenLinks from '../components/ListenLinks.astro';
import EpisodeList from '../components/EpisodeList.astro';
import Credits from '../components/Credits.astro';
import { getEpisodes } from '../lib/episodes';
import { site } from '../site';
import cover from '../assets/cover.jpg';

const episodes = await getEpisodes();
const anyLive = site.listen.some((platform) => platform.url);
const trailer = site.trailerUrl;
const trailerIsAudio = /\.(mp3|m4a|aac|wav|ogg)(\?|$)/i.test(trailer);
---

<Base>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap hero-grid">
      <Image
        src={cover}
        alt="Cover art: a statue of Justice holding scales, over a collage of 1959 newspaper headlines about the Nanavati case."
        widths={[420, 640, 960]}
        sizes="(max-width: 800px) 92vw, 520px"
        class="cover"
        loading="eager"
      />
      <div class="hero-card">
        <p class="kicker">A podcast in four episodes</p>
        <h1>A sensational murder trial. A verdict that outraged a city. The end of the jury in India.</h1>
        <p>
          In 1959, a jury in Bombay acquitted Commander Kawas Nanavati of killing his wife’s lover. Many
          were outraged, and they blamed the jury. Political theorist {site.host.name} follows trial by jury
          from colonial India to the drafting of the Constitution to India’s courts today, and asks what is
          lost when ordinary people no longer decide who is guilty.
        </p>
        {
          trailerIsAudio && (
            <figure class="trailer">
              <figcaption class="kicker">Listen to the trailer</figcaption>
              <audio controls preload="none" src={trailer}>
                <a href={trailer}>Download the trailer</a>
              </audio>
            </figure>
          )
        }
        <div class="actions">
          {trailer && !trailerIsAudio && (
            <a class="btn btn-dark" href={trailer} rel="noopener">▶ Play the trailer</a>
          )}
          {!trailer && (
            <span class="btn btn-dark is-soon">▶ Trailer <small>Soon</small></span>
          )}
          <a class="btn btn-line" href="#listen">Where to listen</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="band-frame">
    <section id="listen" class="band">
      <div class="wrap band-inner">
        <h2>Listen</h2>
        <p>
          {anyLive ? 'Follow the show wherever you get your podcasts.' : 'Coming soon. The links go live with the first episode.'}
        </p>
        <ListenLinks />
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <section class="section paper">
    <div class="wrap case-grid">
      <aside class="case-file" aria-label="Case file">
        <p class="case-file-head">Case file · Bombay, 1959</p>
        <dl>
          <dt>Defendant</dt>
          <dd>Commander Kawas Nanavati, Indian Navy</dd>
          <dt>Charge</dt>
          <dd>Murder</dd>
          <dt>Jury verdict</dt>
          <dd>Not guilty, 8–1</dd>
          <dt>Higher courts</dt>
          <dd>Verdict called “perverse”; Nanavati sent to prison</dd>
          <dt>Trial by jury in India</dt>
          <dd>Abolished, 1973</dd>
        </dl>
        <span class="stamp" aria-hidden="true">Perverse</span>
      </aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="kicker">Why this story</p>
        <h2>What is lost when ordinary people no longer decide?</h2>
        <p>
          In the United States, the right to a trial by jury is easy to take for granted. People grumble
          about jury duty. Yet the jury remains one of the most powerful ways ordinary people can resist a
          state that is punishing someone unfairly.
        </p>
        <p>
          India once had jury trials too. The British introduced them to legitimize their rule as an empire
          of law. But the drafters of the 1950 Constitution, committed to universal suffrage, decided the
          jury was not worth fighting for, and in 1973 it was abolished.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>The Last Jury Trial in India</em> tells that story, and asks what another multicultural
          democracy can teach us about how democratic institutions survive, or decline.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section slate">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The series</p>
      <h2 class="display-heading">Four episodes</h2>
      <EpisodeList episodes={episodes} clamp />
      <p class="after-list">
        <a class="text-link" href="/further-reading/">Further reading for each episode →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section paper">
    <div class="wrap host-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">Your host</p>
        <h2 class="host-name">{site.host.name}</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lede" set:html={site.host.bio} />
        <Credits />
        <p><a class="text-link" href="/about/">More about the series →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
